===Noun===
{{en-noun}} {{tlb|en|American spelling}}

# {{lb|en|military}} The planned [[movement]] of [[troops]], vehicles etc.; a strategic [[repositioning]]; {{i|later also}} a large training field-[[exercise]] of fighting units. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The army was on '''maneuvers'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Joint NATO '''maneuvers''' are as much an exercise in diplomacy as in tactics and logistics.}}
# Any [[strategic]] or [[cunning]] action; a [[stratagem]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1782|author=w:Frances Burney|title=Cecilia|section=III.v.7
|passage=“This,” cried he, “is a '''''manœuvre''''' I have been some time expecting: but Mr. Harrel, though artful and selfish, is by no means deep.”}}
# A [[movement]] of the [[body]], or with an implement, instrument etc., especially one performed with skill or dexterity. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|ease|wrestle}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} A specific medical or surgical movement, often eponymous, done with the doctor's hands or surgical instruments. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The [[otorhinolaryngologist]] performed an Epley '''maneuver''' and the patient was relieved of his vertigo.}}
# A controlled (especially skillful) [[movement]] taken while steering a [[vehicle]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Parallel parking can be a difficult '''maneuver'''.}}

